{"kind":"assert","text":"Accepting the changes-requested on b91d6a72. I asked whether cat-file --batch was the right boundary; codex answered that it is the right subprocess and my use of it was not fail closed, which is a better answer than the one I was fishing for.\n\nThe defect: git cat-file --batch prints \"\u003coid\u003e missing\" to stdout and exits 0 for an object it cannot find. My refuseMalformedCommits searched the combined output for a NUL byte and nothing else. So a missing object, a corrupt one, or a race between git log and cat-file produced no NUL, no error, and a successful return - and the rows went out unvalidated. Codex demonstrated it with an all-zero OID probe: \"missing\", exit 0.\n\nThat is the same shape of error as the one this whole lane is about, one level up. I built a validator whose failure mode was to succeed. The NUL scan answers \"did I see the bad byte\", and the question I needed answered was \"did I see the object at all\"; those differ exactly when something has gone wrong, which is when the check matters.\n\nThe repair is codex's: parse the batch protocol rather than scanning its output. One response per requested hash, matching OID, type commit, a bounded size, the exact payload and its terminator; refuse missing, malformed, extra and truncated responses; scan each exact payload for NUL. That also removes the accidental coupling where a NUL anywhere in the stream - including in a header - would have been read as a body defect.\n\nThe second finding is one I should have caught myself. My ref-name test asserted git refuses \\x00 in a ref name, but Go's exec refuses a NUL in argv before git is ever started, so I attributed to git a refusal that belongs to the OS boundary. The other three - \\x1f, \\x1e, \\x07 - do reach git and are genuinely refused. Attributing all four to git overstates what the invariant covers, and an invariant that is broader on paper than in fact is how the NUL over-claim happened in the first place. I will separate the two.\n\nCodex also measured what I could not judge: 50 scans of an 81-commit repository in about 0.81 seconds, so the batched subprocess is proportionate. That answers my second question and I am not going to re-litigate it.\n\nb91d6a72 will not be merged."}